 "best_diff": "--- initial\n+++ best\n@@ -19,7 +19,32 @@\n     features (they are standardized and a bias is added), so nonlinear structure only\n     helps if a feature expresses it. Only the Python standard library may be used.\n \n-    Current method: the raw inputs.\n+    New method: raw inputs plus engineered nonlinear features.\n     \"\"\"\n-    return list(row)\n+    x1, x2, x3, x4, x5, x6 = row\n+    # Start with raw inputs\n+    out = [x1, x2, x3, x4, x5, x6]\n+    # Pairwise products\n+    out += [x1 * x2, x1 * x6, x2 * x6, x4 * x5]\n+    # Squares of each input\n+    out += [x1 * x1, x2 * x2, x3 * x3, x4 * x4, x5 * x5, x6 * x6]\n+    # Transformations of the skewed variable\n+    out += [math.sqrt(x3), math.log(x3)]\n+    # Sign of pairwise products\n+    out += [1 if x1 * x2 > 0 else -1,\n+            1 if x1 * x6 > 0 else -1,\n+            1 if x2 * x6 > 0 else -1,\n+            1 if x4 * x5 > 0 else -1]\n+    # Indicator thresholds for each variable (except x3)\n+    out += [1 if x1 > 0 else 0,\n+            1 if x2 > 0 else 0,\n+            1 if x4 > 0 else 0,\n+            1 if x5 > 0 else 0,\n+            1 if x6 > 0 else 0]\n+    # Radius in the 3D normal subspace\n+    out += [x1 * x1 + x2 * x2 + x6 * x6]\n+    # Thresholds for the skewed variable\n+    out += [1 if x3 > 1 else 0,\n+            1 if x3 > 2 else 0]\n+    return out\n # OMEGA-BLOCK-END\n",
